        "content":  "================================================================\nUNHRC DRAFT RESOLUTION CLAUSES: AI \u0026 DATA PRIVACY\n================================================================\n\nThis document provides formal, technical preambular and operative clauses suitable for final draft resolutions in the UNHRC.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nFORMAL PREAMBULAR CLAUSES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n• Reaffirming its resolutions HRC 34/7 of 23 March 2017 and HRC 48/4 of 7 October 2021 on the right to privacy in the digital age,\n\n• Reclaiming the sovereign right of each state to regulate domestic communications and security systems to protect territorial integrity, counter-terrorism, and maintain public order in compliance with international law,\n\n• Stressing that the sale, export, and deployment of dual-use surveillance technologies without human rights audits pose an imminent threat to the safety of journalists, human rights defenders, and civil society actors,\n\n• Recognizing with concern that algorithmic bias in machine learning models can result in discriminatory practices in judicial sentencing, housing applications, and public welfare allocation,\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nFORMAL OPERATIVE CLAUSES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n1. Requests the Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR) to compile a comprehensive, biennial report on the human rights implications of generative AI and deep synthesis technologies, with a focus on mitigating political disinformation;\n\n2. Urges member states to mandate Human Rights Impact Assessments (HRIAs) for all high-risk AI applications deployed in law enforcement, border control, and public administration before national licensing is granted;\n\n3. Declares that the deployment of automated, real-time facial recognition in public spaces should be suspended until international human rights standards and independent oversight mechanisms are established;\n\n4. Calls upon states to adopt legal frameworks requiring private tech companies to obtain explicit, opt-in consent before processing biometric data, and to guarantee users the right to delete their profiles;\n\n5. Recommends that the UN establish a Global Digital Sandboxes Network to assist developing nations in testing AI models under secure, privacy-compliant conditions before public deployment;\n\n6. Urges states to implement domestic legislation prohibiting the purchase of personal database registries from unregulated commercial data brokers by state security agencies without prior judicial warrants.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR)\n• UN General Assembly Document Drafting Manual\n================================================================\n"
