        "name":  "Palestine.txt",
        "size":  7767,
        "content":  "================================================================\nPALESTINE\n================================================================\n\nBASIC INFORMATION\n-----------------\nOfficial Name: State of Palestine\nCapital: Jerusalem (Declared) / Ramallah (Administrative)\nGovernment: Parliamentary Democracy (Contested/Provisional Administration)\nCurrent Leader: President Mahmoud Abbas\nPopulation: ~5.4 Million\nUNSC Status: Non-Member Observer State (Active Participant)\nMajor International Organizations: UN, Arab League, OIC, G77, UNESCO\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMAJOR ALLIES\n------------\n• Egypt\n• Qatar\n• Saudi Arabia\n• Jordan\n• Turkey\n• China\n• Russia\n• South Africa\n• OIC Member States\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMAJOR RIVALS\n------------\n• Israel\n• United States\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nNATIONAL INTERESTS\n------------------\n• Securing sovereign maritime rights off the coast of the Gaza Strip.\n• Developing and accessing the Gaza Marine offshore natural gas field.\n• Ending the Israeli blockade of Gaza to establish open trade portals.\n• Rebuilding destroyed civilian infrastructure and supply chain terminals.\n• Securing unhindered international humanitarian supply corridors (food, medicine).\n• Opposing Israeli security controls over Palestinian imports and exports.\n• Promoting international legal accountability for disruptions to civilian trade.\n• Establishing sovereign port and shipping infrastructure in the Mediterranean.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPOSITION ON THE AGENDA\n----------------------\n• Supply Chains: Highlights the complete vulnerability of Palestinian supply chains to Israeli control. Calls for unhindered humanitarian access and sovereign control of borders.\n• Maritime Security: Strongly opposes Israel\u0027s naval blockade of the Gaza Strip, framing it as a collective punishment and violation of international law.\n• Free Trade: Advocates for the right of all peoples to engage in global trade free from military occupations.\n• Sanctions: Rejects unilateral sanctions that exacerbate humanitarian crises or limit the flow of essential civilian goods.\n• International Cooperation: Cooperates via the UN, Arab League, OIC, and G77 to advocate for digital and trade rights.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nHOT TOPICS\n----------\n• Ownership and development rights of the Gaza Marine gas field.\n• Impact of the Israeli maritime blockade on Gaza\u0027s economy and logistics.\n• Security and operation of humanitarian corridors by land and sea.\n• Destruction of civilian logistics infrastructure (ports, warehouses, roads).\n• Palestinian representation and rights under the UNCLOS framework.\n• Trade restrictions on dual-use items imported into Palestinian territories.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSTRENGTHS\n---------\n✓ Broad international recognition in the UN General Assembly and G77.\n✓ Strong moral arguments based on international humanitarian law and UNCLOS.\n✓ Strategic support and aid from wealthy Arab League and OIC states.\n✓ Diplomatic backing from permanent UNSC members (China, Russia).\n✓ High youth literacy and active diaspora supporting digital reconstruction.\n✓ Undersea gas resources (Gaza Marine) representing future economic sovereignty.\n✓ Strong documentation of human rights violations by local CSOs.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWEAKNESSES\n----------\n✗ Complete lack of physical control over territorial waters and borders.\n✗ Division of administrative control between West Bank and Gaza.\n✗ Severe dependency on foreign aid and Israeli-controlled tax transfers.\n✗ Extreme destruction of local manufacturing and logistical capacity.\n✗ No active navy or sovereign merchant shipping fleet.\n✗ Inability to secure direct trade agreements due to occupied status.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY DEFENCE POINTS\n------------------\n• The maritime blockade of Gaza violates Article 33 of the Fourth Geneva Convention.\n• Palestine has sovereign rights to natural resources within its maritime EEZ.\n• Restrictions on import of civilian dual-use items represent collective punishment.\n• Palestine coordinates with international agencies to ensure aid is distributed peacefully.\n• Access to open shipping lanes is a prerequisite for sovereign economic survival.\n• International support is directed toward peaceful, commercial port development.\n• The Gaza Marine gas field belongs to the Palestinian people under international law.\n• State building requires sovereign control over national maritime borders.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nLIKELY QUESTIONS AGAINST THIS COUNTRY\n-------------------------------------\nQ1. How does Palestine propose to manage port security to prevent illegal arms transfers?\nQ2. How does Palestine coordinate with Egypt to manage border trade and transit?\nQ3. What checks prevent humanitarian aid channels from being diverted?\nQ4. How does the political division between Fatah and Hamas affect trade coordination?\nQ5. How does Palestine address security concerns raised by Israel regarding open port access?\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nQUESTIONS THIS COUNTRY CAN ASK\n------------------------------\nAsk one strong diplomatic question each to:\n• Israel: When will Israel lift the maritime blockade of Gaza to allow civilian trade?\n• USA: How does the US justify supporting maritime blockades that cause food insecurity?\n• Egypt: How can we coordinate to secure humanitarian corridors during conflicts?\n• China: How can China support infrastructural development of Palestinian ports?\n• Russia: What steps will Russia take to support food security in Palestine?\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nRED LINES\n----------\nThe country is unlikely to support:\n• Resolutions that validate Israel\u0027s control over Gaza\u0027s territorial waters.\n• Any framework that permits the blockade of civilian trade under security pretexts.\n• Rejection of Palestine\u0027s right to develop the Gaza Marine gas field.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nNEGOTIATION STRATEGY\n--------------------\n• Countries to ally with: Arab League, OIC, South Africa, Ireland, China, Russia, G77.\n• Countries to avoid confronting first: Gulf states (maintain coordination on aid).\n• Possible compromises: Support international third-party inspections for Gaza port cargo.\n• Main objectives: Condemn blockades, assert maritime EEZ rights, secure humanitarian aid.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMUN TIPS\n--------\n✓ Speak with high emotional and moral weight, focusing on humanitarian law.\n✓ Frame the blockade as a barrier to global free trade and civilian survival.\n✓ Reference specific international treaties (Geneva Conventions, UNCLOS).\n✓ Align closely with China and G77 statements on sovereign development.\n✓ Deflect security queries by proposing third-party international monitors.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\n----------------\n• United Nations Security Council (UNSC)\n• Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Expatriates of Palestine\n• Palestinian Central Bureau of Statistics (PCBS)\n• United Nations Relief and Works Agency (UNRWA)\n• UNCTAD Trade and Development Reports\n• Amnesty International Reports on Blockades\n================================================================"
