        "content":  "================================================================\nUNSC SOLUTIONS PORTFOLIO: MARITIME SECURITY \u0026 TRADE\n================================================================\n\nThis document details four debate-ready solutions for resolutions addressing maritime safety and global supply chains.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nSOLUTION 1: UN MARITIME SECURITY COORDINATION MECHANISM\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: Establishing a centralized, UN-coordinated system to bridge communications between different naval coalitions (e.g., Operation Prosperity Guardian, Operation Aspides, and non-Western patrols) and commercial shipping.\n• Key Elements:\n  - Real-time transit tracking and threat alerts.\n  - Joint protocols for responding to distress signals in conflict zones.\n  - De-confliction zones near major choke points.\n• Bloc Support: Supported by transit states and Western allies.\n• Opposed by: States concerned with foreign intelligence access to shipping databases.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nSOLUTION 2: SHADOW FLEET SAFETY MORATORIUM\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: Requiring all vessels carrying hazardous cargo (such as crude oil) through international straits to verify P\u0026I insurance and compliance with IMO structural safety rules (double-hull requirements).\n• Key Elements:\n  - Coastal state inspections of non-compliant vessels at strait entrances.\n  - Centralized UN registry of verified, insured tankers.\n  - Establishing environmental response teams in high-risk zones.\n• Bloc Support: Sanctions-enforcing Western states.\n• Opposed by: Russia and states operating shadow fleet logistics.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nSOLUTION 3: UNDERSEA INFRASTRUCTURE PROTECTION PROTOCOLS\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: Creating a joint monitoring framework to protect undersea fiber-optic communication cables and gas pipelines in international waters.\n• Key Elements:\n  - Sound sensor arrays and satellite tracking of suspicious vessels lingering over cables.\n  - Rapid-repair agreements to deploy technicians during sabotage events.\n  - Sovereign control over maintenance remains within EEZs.\n• Bloc Support: Broad consensus, though states reject sharing sensitive naval operations data.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nSOLUTION 4: TRADE CORRIDOR DIVERSIFICATION AND CAPACITY BUILDING\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: Supporting the development of alternative transport corridors (such as the Middle Corridor or North-South Corridor) to de-risk global trade from single choke point bottlenecks.\n• Key Elements:\n  - Financial support from the World Bank to modernize rail networks in Central Asia.\n  - Simplification of border customs protocols through digital registries.\n  - Strengthening port efficiency in developing transit states.\n• Bloc Support: Supported by Global South, landlocked Central Asian states, and China.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• International Maritime Organization (IMO) Conventions\n• UNCTAD Maritime Transport Studies\n================================================================\n"
