        "path":  "UNSC/Resources/Case Studies.txt",
        "name":  "Case Studies.txt",
        "size":  3481,
        "content":  "================================================================\nUNSC RESOURCE GUIDE: GLOBAL CASE STUDIES\n================================================================\n\nReference these case studies to support your arguments regarding maritime security and supply chain vulnerabilities.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nCASE STUDY 1: THE RED SEA HOUTHI ATTACKS (2023-PRESENT)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: Following geopolitical conflict in Gaza, Yemeni Houthi forces launched drone and missile attacks on commercial vessels transiting the Bab-el-Mandeb Strait, declaring a blockade on Israel-bound shipping.\n• Impact:\n  - Forced major shipping firms (Maersk, MSC) to divert ships around Africa.\n  - Caused a 40% reduction in Suez Canal transit revenues, severely affecting Egypt\u0027s economy.\n  - Led to the establishment of US-led Operation Prosperity Guardian and EU Operation Aspides.\n• Debate Use: Cite to argue for international task forces to protect trade lanes and coordinate navigation security.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nCASE STUDY 2: NORD STREAM PIPELINE SABOTAGE (2022)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: Underwater explosions ruptured three of the four Nord Stream natural gas pipelines in the Baltic Sea within Denmark and Sweden\u0027s EEZ, releasing massive amounts of methane.\n• Impact:\n  - Destroyed direct gas delivery routes from Russia to Germany, forcing a rapid energy supply chain shift to LNG imports.\n  - Highlighted the extreme vulnerability of undersea critical energy and data infrastructure.\n• Debate Use: Cite to demand coordinated UN-led protection for undersea cables and pipelines in international waters.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nCASE STUDY 3: THE BLACK SEA GRAIN INITIATIVE (2022-2023)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: A UN and Turkey-brokered agreement between Russia and Ukraine to permit the safe transit of agricultural exports from three Ukrainian Black Sea ports.\n• Impact:\n  - Allowed over 32 million metric tons of grain to reach global markets, reducing global food prices by 20%.\n  - Terminated by Russia in July 2023, who cited obstacles to its own agricultural and fertilizer exports.\n• Debate Use: Cite to prove that neutral mediation and UN-brokered trade corridors are effective alternatives to military escorts.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nCASE STUDY 4: THE SUEZ CANAL BLOCKAGE (2021)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Description: The ultra-large container ship Ever Given ran aground and blocked the Suez Canal for six days, halting over 400 ships.\n• Impact:\n  - Blocked an estimated $9.6 billion worth of trade daily.\n  - Demonstrated the high risk of single choke point dependencies.\n• Debate Use: Cite when arguing for trade corridor diversification and investment in alternate routes (such as Central Asian rail links).\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• UNCTAD Trade and Development Reports\n• International Maritime Organization Safety Incident Circulars\n• World Bank Food Security Updates\n================================================================\n"
