        "path":  "UNSC/Resources/Maritime Law.txt",
        "name":  "Maritime Law.txt",
        "size":  3116,
        "content":  "================================================================\nUNSC RESOURCE GUIDE: KEY MARITIME LAW CONVENTIONS\n================================================================\n\nThis document outlines key conventions in international maritime law that govern strait transit, shipping safety, and pollution prevention.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n1. THE MONTREUX CONVENTION (1936)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Key Focus: Regulates transit through the Turkish Straits (Bosporus and Dardanelles).\n• Key Rules:\n  - Guarantees complete freedom of passage for civilian vessels in peace and war.\n  - Limits the tonnage and duration of stay for non-Black Sea military warships (maximum 21 days, maximum 15,000 tons per vessel).\n  - Grants Turkey the authority to close the straits to all foreign warships during times of war or when threatened.\n• Debate Use: Crucial for debates on Black Sea security, grain corridors, and regional naval deployments.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n2. THE SUA CONVENTION (1988)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Title: Convention for the Suppression of Unlawful Acts against the Safety of Maritime Navigation.\n• Key Focus: Suppressing maritime terrorism, hijackings, and boarding of civilian ships.\n• Key Rules:\n  - Defines offenses including seizure of ships by force, acts of violence against persons on board, and placing destructive devices on ships.\n  - Requires state parties to prosecute or extradite suspects found in their territory.\n• Debate Use: Cite to argue for international legal accountability for Houthi attacks on merchant ships.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n3. THE SOLAS CONVENTION (1974)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Title: International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea.\n• Key Focus: Minimum safety standards for merchant ship construction, equipment, and operation.\n• Key Rules:\n  - Requires commercial ships to carry active Automatic Identification System (AIS) transponders.\n  - Turning off AIS to hide shipping movements (common in shadow fleets) violates SOLAS safety rules.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n4. THE MARPOL CONVENTION (1973)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Title: International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships.\n• Key Focus: Preventing marine pollution from operational or accidental causes.\n• Key Rules:\n  - Regulates discharges of oil, chemicals, and sewage.\n  - Older, poorly maintained shadow fleet tankers operating without oil-spill liability insurance violate MARPOL guidelines.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• International Maritime Organization (IMO) Treaty Database\n• Montreux Convention Treaty texts (1936)\n================================================================\n"
