        "path":  "UNSC/Resources/UNCLOS.txt",
        "name":  "UNCLOS.txt",
        "size":  3357,
        "content":  "================================================================\nUNSC RESOURCE GUIDE: UNCLOS NAVIGATION \u0026 TRANSIT RIGHTS\n================================================================\n\nThis document reviews the primary articles of the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) governing commercial transit and maritime security.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n1. ARTICLE 17 - RIGHT OF INNOCENT PASSAGE\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Summary: Ships of all states, whether coastal or land-locked, enjoy the right of innocent passage through the territorial sea (up to 12 nautical miles from baseline).\n• Key Constraint:\n  - Passage is innocent so long as it is not prejudicial to the peace, good order, or security of the coastal state.\n  - Activities like military drilling, surveillance, or pollution violate innocent passage.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n2. ARTICLE 38 - RIGHT OF TRANSIT PASSAGE\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Summary: In straits used for international navigation (e.g., Strait of Hormuz, Strait of Malacca), all ships and aircraft enjoy the right of transit passage.\n• Key Constraint:\n  - Transit passage is the exercise of freedom of navigation solely for the purpose of continuous and expeditious transit.\n  - Coastal states border-lining the straits cannot suspend or restrict transit passage.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n3. ARTICLE 56 - RIGHTS IN THE EXCLUSIVE ECONOMIC ZONE (EEZ)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Summary: In the EEZ (up to 200 nautical miles), the coastal state has sovereign rights for exploring and managing natural resources (fish, gas).\n• Key Constraint:\n  - Other states retain freedoms of navigation, overflight, and laying undersea cables (Article 58).\n  - Subject of debate regarding whether foreign navies can conduct military drills in another state\u0027s EEZ without notice.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n4. ARTICLE 87 - FREEDOM OF THE HIGH SEAS\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Summary: The high seas are open to all states. Freedoms include navigation, overflight, laying undersea cables, fishing, and scientific research.\n• Key Constraint:\n  - High seas must be reserved for peaceful purposes (Article 88).\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n5. ARTICLE 110 - RIGHT OF VISIT\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• Summary: A warship may board a foreign merchant ship on the high seas if there is reasonable ground for suspecting that the ship is engaged in piracy, slave trade, unauthorized broadcasting, or is without nationality.\n• Key Constraint:\n  - Does not authorize boarding based solely on cargo destination or unilateral sanctions compliance without specific UN security mandates.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (1982)\n• Division for Ocean Affairs and the Law of the Sea (DOALOS)\n================================================================\n"
